(window.IBALL_PERIODICAL_LIBRARY = window.IBALL_PERIODICAL_LIBRARY || {})["2026-09-14"] = {"meta":{"id":"2026-09-14","label":"2026 年 9 月 14 日 - 9 月 20 日","date":"2026-09-14","range":"2026-09-14|2026-09-20","theme":"文化","themes":["文化"],"sources":["The Guardian","BARRON’S","Economist"],"title":"Book explores Edna O’Brien’s twilight","titleZh":"（9.14—9.20）","counts":{"article":7,"paragraph":12,"vocab":64,"question":20,"qa":0,"original":7,"layout":1},"tabs":{"articles":true,"reading":true,"tests":true,"qa":false,"originals":true}},"articles":[{"file":"9.14—9.20 原文(1).docx","articles":[{"title":"Book explores Edna O’Brien’s twilight years","titleZh":"","source":"The Guardian","publishedOn":"9.14","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"After visiting his dying mother in London, Carlo Gébler returned to his rural home in Northern Ireland one night and encountered a hedgehog sitting on the doormat. A thought bubbled up: his mother had died and been reincarnated as this woodland creature."},{"index":2,"text":"In fact, his mother, the novelist Edna O’Brien, was still alive, but his mind insisted otherwise. “At that moment that’s what I believed. And then of course I stopped believing it because I knew it wasn’t true.” When O’Brien died days later in July 2024, at the age of 93, the literary world mourned the passing of a singular voice who had scandalised her native Ireland with depictions of female sexuality and blazed a trail for Irish writers."},{"index":3,"text":"Now her son has produced a chronicle of O’Brien’s final years, a twilight marked by her struggle to keep working despite illness and frailty, and of the impact of her death on his own life. Published this Friday, No One Tells You: The Last Years of Edna O’Brien documents the writer’s doomed battle to complete a final book about TS Eliot as age and infirmity sapped her powers."},{"index":4,"text":"O’Brien escaped a stifling and conservative upbringing in County Clare by moving to Dublin and eloping with Ernest Gébler, an Irish-Czech writer, to England, where they raised two sons, Carlo and Sasha, before divorcing. The Country Girls, published in 1960, established her reputation, launching her image as a glamorous public persona in London: a convent-educated rebel who befriended Hollywood stars while writing novels, biographies, plays and poetry."},{"index":5,"text":"Female desire and subversion populated her fiction decades before Ireland liberalised and legalised divorce, same-sex marriage and abortion, but that did not entitle Irish people to “co-opt” her memory for political ends, said Gébler. “In Ireland people want a narrative which emphasises the progressive journey, as if she planned all the political progress on the back of an envelope."},{"index":6,"text":"Gébler said his mother endured many hostile reviews, initially by critics who deemed her work “unseemly” and later by others who disliked her forays into the lives of historical figures such as James Joyce’s muse and wife, Nora Barnacle, or the kidnapping of girls in Nigeria. “Her fiction changed its register, but the world isn’t very sympathetic to that. It’s kind of: ‘It’s not your gig, madam.’ ”"},{"index":7,"text":"The posthumous acclaim for his mother validated the morbid joke about death being a great career move, said Gébler. “A living writer is sometimes overlooked because there’s so much other noise, but death is an even bigger noise, so you can get people’s distracted attention.”"},{"index":8,"text":"For Gébler, however, her passing brought loss, confusion and dread. “The awful thing about a parent’s death is that you move up to the cliff edge. Once they’re gone, you know you’re next. And then you start to have all sorts of terrible thoughts. Why did I do this to my children? They didn’t ask to be born and then they too are going to die.”"},{"index":9,"text":"The impulse to write the memoir came in her final weeks as he shuttled from his home in County Fermanagh to tend her in London. “The unconscious is very patient and very clever, and it always waits until the right moment. And then comes this very strong sense: right, this is what I must write.”"},{"index":10,"text":"The finality of death was unconscionable, said Gébler. “There’s no way around the fact: one’s own extinction is disgusting to contemplate, as is the extinction of other people.”"},{"index":11,"text":"Those close to a dying person had an obligation to let them feel seen and heard, he said. “Human being to human being, you listen. That’s your most important duty.”"},{"index":12,"text":"His other advice is to impose order on the chaos of loss by writing about it, whether in scribbled notes or a published book. “If you just let things fester in the basement of the psyche, they just get bigger and darker and huger and more and more terrible. You have to get it up into the light. And then you can come to some sort of accommodation with it, or a truce.”"}]},{"title":"Families scramble to secure Send plans ahead of ministers’ reforms","titleZh":"","source":"The Guardian","publishedOn":"9.15","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"Schools and councils in England are expecting the proportion of children with special needs plans to double to one in 10, as families rush to secure support before changes come in. Headteachers have reported an “unreal” surge in applications for education, health and care plans (EHCPs) mandating support for needs or disabilities such as speech and learning difficulties, autism or ADHD. In 2025, just one in 20 school-age children had EHCPs."},{"index":2,"text":"The number of pupils with EHCPs has risen by more than 11% in each of the last two years, and projections by local authorities seen by the Guardian are anticipating double-digit annual increases over the next three years. Similar levels nationally would translate to almost 10% of all school-age children having an EHCP in 2029."},{"index":3,"text":"Such a rapid rise would outstrip the government’s own forecasts and eat into funding for improving specialist support in mainstream schools, undermining plans for reform while straining school and council budgets. School leaders said special educational needs (Send) coordinators had worked through the summer to deal with the influx of requests for assessments, as secondary schools reported that many more year 7 students were arriving with EHCPs or applications."},{"index":4,"text":"Bill Revans, the County Councils Network’s spokesperson on special educational needs and disabilities as well as Liberal Democrat leader of Somerset council, said: “It is clear there has been a particularly sharp surge in assessment requests to our member councils this year since the government announced its planned reforms in February. Our previous analysis had predicted the number of EHCPs will rise to 840,000 by 2028-29; however, it is now likely these numbers will be exceeded."},{"index":5,"text":"EHCPs are legal documents agreed between local authorities and families in England detailing the support required for children and young people up to the age of 25, allowing access to extra funding or places in special schools. While more than 530,000 school-age children have EHCPs, an additional 1.3 million have identified special needs, according to official data."},{"index":6,"text":"The costs are met out of local authority high needs budgets but the huge growth in EHCPs has led to councils amassing sustained deficits, requiring a statutory override granted by central government to avoid many becoming technically bankrupt. Georgia Gould, the schools minister overseeing the changes, said the government was carefully considering the responses to its white paper, which aims to give children earlier diagnosis and intervention, and moderate the need for EHCPs."},{"index":7,"text":"The white paper’s timeline would result in more children with individual support plans alongside increased support in mainstream schools; while EHCPs would be designed for those with more profound needs and disabilities from 2030."},{"index":8,"text":"During a visit to Thomas Lord Audley secondary school in Colchester, Gould said parts of the planned changes would begin operating this year, including the government’s new “experts in hand” scheme that puts specialists directly into mainstream schools and nurseries. “I’ve spoken to parents around the country, and lots of families and children have been really let down by the system,” Gould said. “So they are really concerned, and any change can feel worrying for those families.”"},{"index":9,"text":"“That’s why we want to put in place tangible change they can see and feel in classrooms and in their communities. And we’ve been clear we will build a new and inclusive system before we bring in wider changes.” Gould acknowledged it was “going to take time to build that trust, to work with families for them to see that day to day. All the work we are doing, the investment, the teacher training, the sharing of best practice, is so important.”"},{"index":10,"text":"Paul Whiteman, the general secretary of the National Association of Head Teachers, said the reports of increases in EHCPs chimed with “what we hear from school leaders”. Whiteman said: “While it is hard to give a definitive reason for why this is happening, it appears likely that the government’s plans to restrict future EHCPs to children with the most complex needs may have led to more families and schools applying before these changes kick in.”"},{"index":11,"text":"The surge in applications has led some local authorities to dramatically raise their own forecasts. A report by Hampshire council in July showed applications so far this year were at record levels and forecast a 58% rise in EHCPs between 2026 and 2030. Manchester council is also anticipating a 40% increase in school-age children with EHCPs by 2029."}]},{"title":"This Start-Up Is More Something Than Nothing","titleZh":"","source":"BARRON’S","publishedOn":"9.16","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"Artificial intelligence has upended Wall Street, Main Street, and every road in between. In Silicon Valley, venture capital dollars are rushing to find AI targets, with total venture funding surging to $256 billion in the first quarter of 2026, according to PitchBook, surpassing the 2025 total."},{"index":2,"text":"As with public markets, those AI dollars have crowded out other investments, including the once red-hot area of consumer tech. After peaking near 3,000 in 2021, the number of VC deals for consumer tech was cut in half by last year, PitchBook data show, and the figure continues to slide in 2026."},{"index":3,"text":"Blame the decline on the do-everything smartphone, which fills in as a camera, GPS, digital assistant, calculator, and all sorts of other products that once filled the aisles of an electronics shop. Jeff Barrington, managing director at Windsor Drake, tells Barron’s that building a consumer tech start-up is incredibly challenging. Margins are low, supply challenges are vast, and competition is fierce. “The other thing, too, is that the big Apples, Amazons, and Googles tend to copy or absorb the consumer tech products,” he says."},{"index":4,"text":"That history makes it all the more surprising that one of this year’s hottest consumer tech start-ups is taking on the establishment with…a phone. Nothing, founded in 2020, began as an earbud maker before moving into smartphones and smartwatches. The products have a clean and modern aesthetic that has attracted a young audience looking to break away from tech giants Apple and Samsung Electronics. Its prices often undercut the competition. The Nothing 4a Pro phone with 256GB of storage starts at $599; a comparable iPhone17 goes for $799."},{"index":5,"text":"The company, which didn’t make executives available for interview, crossed $1 billion in lifetime sales in 2024. Nothing has garnered a lot of social-media attention, with Google trends for “Nothing Phone” and “Nothing headphones” both up 500% versus the previous five years."},{"index":6,"text":"The company is a regular presence on TikTok, where users gloat about their choice of a non-Apple device. Nothing, itself, isn’t shy about taking on the tech behemoth. “This is a message to Apple,” said CEO Carl Pei in a video posted to his TikTok account in June. “My name is Carl. I make phones in London. And I’m gonna steal your customers. One bored iPhone user at a time.”"},{"index":7,"text":"In a fairly commoditized world of smartphones, Nothing has tried to stand out with at least one unusual feature: a rear display through which users can deliver notifications with various symbols and animations. The idea is to keep the phone facedown, limiting overall screen time."},{"index":8,"text":"Nothing has raised $456 million, per PitchBook, including a Series C funding round that brought in $200 million last year, giving the company a valuation of $1.3 billion. It’s rare air for consumer tech firms. According to PitchBook, of more than 31,000 VC-funded consumertech companies, just 0.4% have generated a $1 billion-plus exit. Even for those, it’s often a tough go."},{"index":9,"text":"Fitbit, the wearable fitness-tracking device firm founded in 2007, raised about $66 million in VC funding before going public in 2015 with a $4.1 billion market value. Six years later, the firm sold itself to Alphabet’s Google for half that amount."},{"index":10,"text":"Meanwhile, this past week marked the end of a long saga for GoPro, the maker of cameras for outdoor enthusiasts founded in 2002. GoPro went public in 2014 with a market value of $3 billion, just in time for the smartphone camera to begin eating into its sales. For months, GoPro shares have traded below $1. On Tuesday, the company said it was being acquired by Starman Optical in a $285 million deal that would expand the business into the “AI data center, government, defense, and aerospace markets.”"},{"index":11,"text":"Venture capitalists are nothing if not optimists, though. Mark Vena, CEO and principal analyst at SmartTechResearch, tells Barron’s that VC investors are currently looking for the next big consumer hardware AI play. He believes that the next consumertech winner will be an AI-powered wearable device that can potentially listen to a person’s day-to-day happenings, and help with anything from scheduling appointments, messaging loved ones, or sending a reminder about picking up groceries on the way home after work."},{"index":12,"text":"The challenge for Nothing will be merging its plain hardware aesthetic—the inspiration for its brand name—with the complications and baggage of AI. According to an August study from the Pew Research Center, 55% of adults under age 30 now say they’re more concerned than excited about AI."},{"index":13,"text":"Nothing is betting that audience just needs a different device. “For AI to reach its full potential, consumer hardware must reinvent itself,” the company says on its website. September 7, 2026 | BARRON’S"}]},{"title":"No more excuses","titleZh":"","source":"Economist","publishedOn":"9.17","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"Schools in RICH countries are failing. The latest scores on PISA tests of reading, maths and science, released on September 8th, since the tests, administered by the OECD, began in 2000. Educators can no longer blame it all on covid-19 lockdowns, which badly messed up lessons and caused PISA grades to drop. The pandemic is now over, but scores have continued to fall roughly as fast. In 35 rich countries that take part in the tests, a typical 15-year-old now reads no better than a 14-year-old did a decade ago. A slow-motion disaster is under way."},{"index":2,"text":"Test scores are not the full measure of a child. But they are a good predictor of desirable outcomes and a bulwark against many of the worst ones. Kids who do well on tests tend to earn more, live longer and healthier lives, and commit fewer crimes. By one estimate, pushing up PISA scores by a mere quarter of a standard deviation might increase annual growth in rich countries by half a percentage point."},{"index":3,"text":"Perhaps, though, as artificial intelligence improves, the fact that children struggle intellectually will not matter so much? The adults of the future will be able to outsource their thinking to clever machines, just as they use machines to perform innumerable physical tasks. So why sweat about swotting?"},{"index":4,"text":"This argument could not be more wrong. AI will not make it pointless to train human brains, any more than the invention of cars eliminated the need for physical exercise. On the contrary, adapting to a world of rapid, AI-driven change will require more mental agility, not less. And precisely because AI will increase the temptation for people to outsource difficult cognitive tasks, schools have an extra duty to help pupils exercise their brains and acquire the hard habits of thinking. Failure to furnish them with the basic building blocks of a well-developed mind, such as literacy and numeracy, is inexcusable."},{"index":5,"text":"PISA scores began drifting downwards around 2012. The evidence is growing that tablets, mobile phones and other such screens are distracting youngsters from their studies and diverting them from hobbies, like reading books, that instil the ability to focus. So limits on screens seem prudent. About half of countries now ban mobile phones from schools to some extent; more should follow. Used carefully, technology can benefit classrooms. More usually, snazzy new tools and devices just get in the way."},{"index":6,"text":"An even bigger problem, though, is muddle-headed thinking among educators. Some have fallen for the leftist canard that tests are harmful and grades are racist. Some have allowed reasonable worries about children’s mental health to warp into an excuse for low expectations. Some unhelpful habits acquired during the pandemic have stuck: children are still missing more lessons than before, and many teachers who relaxed their grading standards have failed to tighten up again."},{"index":7,"text":"What to do? There is little evidence that shovelling more cash into schools will make much difference: Japan spends 30% less per pupil than America and gets much better results. But research needs a big boost. Teaching, as a profession, remains shamefully uninterested in the evidence about which techniques work best. Education departments in universities contain some of the weakest and most radical staff, often keener on abstract ideas of social justice than the more concrete benefits that better grades might bring to pupils."},{"index":8,"text":"Places that have avoided declines deserve more attention. Britain is one. In 2009 its pupils scraped into the world’s top 30 in maths and reading. Now they sit in the top ten. Some of this stems from a return in England, under a previous Conservative government, to old-fashioned things such as rigorous exams, tough inspections and fact-filled curriculums."},{"index":9,"text":"Falling scores will not be fixed in a jiffy. Because it takes time to educate a child, the latest test results bear the marks of decisions taken ten to 20 years ago. That long lag makes reform politically harder. Governments that make schools better will seldom see benefits they can boast about before the next election. But they should care anyway. Politicians in past decades, from Tony Blair to George Bush, have won office promising to fix education. Today’s leaders seem to have cooled on schools, except as culture-war battlegrounds. That lack of serious attention invites calamity. As machines grow brighter, don’t let teenagers grow any dimmer. September 12TH-18TH ｜Leaders"}]},{"title":"Silicon dunes","titleZh":"","source":"Economist","publishedOn":"9.18","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"Soon after war broke out in the Gulf, Iranian drones targeted Amazon’s data centres in the United Arab Emirates (UAE) and Bahrain. But at Stargate UAE, a 1-gigawatt data centre being built on a 5GW campus in Abu Dhabi, work “never stopped,” says an executive at a state-backed artificial-intelligence firm. The first 200-megawatt phase is almost complete."},{"index":2,"text":"Last year PwC, a consultancy, predicted that across the Gulf data-centre capacity would rise fourfold within five years, to about 4GW. The outbreak of war has not halted the boom. HUMAIN, an AI company established by Saudi Arabia’s sovereign-wealth fund, announced last month that it would build a facility with DataVolt, another Saudi firm, at Oxagon, an industrial city on the country’s west coast. Two other Saudi outfits, Axelerated Solutions and Al Moammar Information Systems, have also trumpeted new data-centre investments."},{"index":3,"text":"America’s hyperscalers, too, seem to have lost none of their enthusiasm. In September Amazon Web Services said it was expanding a partnership with HUMAIN to provide more data-centre capacity by 2028. Last month Microsoft, which has leased Saudi sites from Al Moammar and is working with Du, an Emirati telecom firm, to build a data centre in the UAE, said it would bring more regional capacity online in November."},{"index":4,"text":"Several factors explain the region’s continued attraction, despite the conflict. One is cheap energy: electricity tariffs in the Gulf are around six cents per kilowatt hour, compared with between nine and 15 cents in America, reports PwC. Vast amounts of renewable power and new transmission infrastructure have pushed costs lower. Another is cheap land: according to PwC, industrial land for data centres in Saudi Arabia costs $10-50 per square metre, well below the $150-600 in northern Virginia, where many of America’s server farms live. McKinsey, another consultancy, puts the cost of building and powering a 100-megawatt AI data centre in the Gulf at $200-220 per MW hour, nearly as low as in China."},{"index":5,"text":"The Gulf has also established itself as a hub for industry talent: last year the UAE and Saudi Arabia were among the countries that recorded the highest net inflow of AI workers relative to their workforces. There is no evidence that war has sent this flow of talent into reverse."},{"index":6,"text":"Then there are the Gulf states’ deep pockets. In July MGX, an Emirati state-run fund, raised $50bn to invest in AI. Most of that money will flow to overseas companies, but those wanting a slice of it will be eager to keep their paymasters happy by investing in the region."},{"index":7,"text":"Domestic policies in the Gulf will require more data centres, in effect underwriting demand. In April the UAE said it would deploy “agentic AI” across half of the government. The country’s National Cloud Security Policy requires “operational sovereignty” for government entities and operators of critical infrastructure, further supporting the build-out, while Saudi Arabia’s financial institutions are generally required to use cloud services within the country. Luca Bennici of McKinsey notes that private firms in the Gulf have “growing concerns” of their own about data protection and sovereignty."},{"index":8,"text":"What is more, there is little of the public opposition to data centres increasingly on show in the West, where many people resent power-hungry facilities on their doorstep. Bashing data centres has turned into a popular campaign strategy in American politics. In the Gulf, not so much. “Centralised decision making” helps, says an executive in the region."},{"index":9,"text":"True, the war has disrupted the Gulf’s carefully crafted economic boom. It has also made the region’s rulers more determined to make their digital investments secure. Kurt Davis junior of Alvarez & Marsal, one more consultancy, notes that plans to make data centres as safe as embassies, even putting them underground, are being mooted. Nevertheless, the build-out continues. September 12TH-18TH ｜Business"}]},{"title":"Taking the hit","titleZh":"","source":"Economist","publishedOn":"9.19","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"Hurtling around Earth at roughly seven kilometres a second, even tiny pieces of debris pack quite a punch. Such manmade junk poses a far greater threat to satellites circling in low orbits than natural space rocks do—and the risks are rising. According to the European Space Agency, the amount of rubbish in orbit has more than doubled since 2017."},{"index":2,"text":"Roughly one small satellite in five stops working within a year of launch, estimates a study published in 2021, and in three-fifths of these cases operators do not know what happened. The smart money is on quite a few such failures being caused by collisions with space debris. To provide more data Odin Space, a firm based in Santa Ana, California, has begun selling a system that uses vibration-sensing adhesive strips to detect and assess impacts. The results should help settle bets on the best shielding for a given orbit."},{"index":3,"text":"One long-standing approach to protecting satellites is a Whipple shield—a two-stage affair which places an outlying bumper a few centimetres above a main shield, both of which are typically made of aluminium. The bumper breaks an incoming object into a spray of smaller, less energetic fragments that the main shield can then stop more easily. But this arrangement is bulky and heavy. So most satellites simply take their chances behind aluminium panels stiffened with a honeycomb also made of that metal. These offer only limited protection from debris."},{"index":4,"text":"Yet the range of options for protecting satellites is increasing. One of the alternatives on offer is composite tiles. Atomic-6, a firm near Atlanta, is developing these under the brand name Space Armor. It is cagey about the materials involved, but the resulting tiles are hand-size, 2.5cm thick and weigh about as much as an iPhone. In tests, they successfully stop aluminium projectiles the size of a pea that have been accelerated by a gas gun to 7.2km a second. Moreover, unlike Whipple shields, they throw off no shrapnel that could become someone else’s problem, says Trevor Smith, Atomic-6’s boss. Instead, incoming metal objects (and much space junk is metallic) are vaporised and absorbed."},{"index":5,"text":"Other types of shielding are also advancing. One combines Kevlar, a fibre used to make bullet-proof jackets (and which also helps protect the International Space Station), with Nextel, a woven ceramic. This promises light shields for smaller, less expensive spacecraft—weight being a crucial consideration for launch costs."},{"index":6,"text":"Another approach, from a team at the University of Padua, in Italy, employs 3D printing to up the ante. Lorenzo Olivieri and his colleagues have made experimental shields using aluminium, Kevlar and resin reinforced with carbon fibres. Printing such shields layer by layer allows internal cavities to be introduced. These voids, says Dr Olivieri, act as small Whipple shields, subjecting incoming projectiles to successive shocks that serve to break them into ever-tinier fragments."},{"index":7,"text":"A still-further means to deliver Whipple-mimicking repeated shocks is with aluminium foam. This is made by packing a ceramic material into a porous plastic structure, then burning away the plastic to leave a network of cavities into which molten aluminium is poured. Knocking the ceramic out leaves behind a metal matrix that has voids up to 5mm in diameter scattered throughout its interior. Testing by NASA, America’s space agency, found that a shield incorporating a layer of this foam a little over 6mm thick could provide the same protection as a conventional Whipple shield of twice the weight."},{"index":8,"text":"However promising this repertoire of shielding types is, though, much will hinge on what they encounter. Projectiles range from chunks of metal and pieces of plastic to rocks and flecks of paint, and the mix varies with the orbit, notes Rannveig Marie Faergestad, a shielding designer at Thales Alenia Space, a Franco-Italian spacecraft-maker. Better knowledge of what prevails in a given zone would therefore help, and Odin Space’s impact sensors should provide it. That will make girding for orbit a bit less of a shot in the dark. September 12TH-18TH ｜Science & technology"}]},{"title":"The Navier-Stokes problem","titleZh":"","source":"Economist","publishedOn":"9.20","paragraphs":[{"index":1,"text":"To mark the turn of the century the Clay Mathematics Institute, in Denver, took stock of what mathematicians knew and what they did not. The result was a set of seven Millennium Prize problems representing the most important unsolved matters in maths. Solutions would attract a purse of $1m."},{"index":2,"text":"Until September 8th only one of these problems had been solved. On that day, however, OpenAI announced it had conquered another. This is called the Navier-Stokes existence and smoothness problem, and it relates to a set of equations that capture the flow of fluids. Mathematicians have been trying to bottle this one up for at least a century. OpenAI says a team of its AI agents solved it in just 88 hours."},{"index":3,"text":"The Navier-Stokes equations are useful for modelling how air flows over aircraft wings, oil squeezes through pipes and plasma circulates inside stars. But, though mathematicians have a good grasp of how to harness them practically, a thorough understanding of the properties of the equations themselves eludes them."},{"index":4,"text":"This is where the Millennium problem comes in. It asks “whether the Navier-Stokes equations can malfunction in a finite amount of time when describing a fluid of fixed density moving in three dimensions”. Such a malfunction would come in the form of a singularity: a point where a quantity, such as the fluid’s speed, becomes infinite and the equations break down. So far, mathematicians have been unable to prove such singularities never exist, but have also failed to find one."},{"index":5,"text":"Now, OpenAI claims its AI agents have found one. On August 28th the firm’s scientists began training a new model. They spawned teams of AI agents, powered by this model, that can read the internet, run code and communicate with one another. On September 1st they set them to work on the open Millennium problems."},{"index":6,"text":"Fifty hours later, a team of around 100 agents answered a question closely related to the Navier-Stokes problem. Noting this as a stepping-stone to a full solution, the scientists redirected their swarm of autonomous mathematicians to focus only on Navier-Stokes. On September 5th, after 88 hours of work, 2.7m messages and the consumption of at least $6.5m-worth of computing resources, a team of around 10,000 agents discovered a solution: a swirling vortex of spinning fluid with a velocity that grows uncontrollably to form a singularity."},{"index":7,"text":"With the Millennium problem allegedly solved, should OpenAI expect a cheque in the post? On that point, the situation is as chaotic as the equations themselves."},{"index":8,"text":"The firm says it began its work after hearing a rumour that Tristan Buckmaster, a mathematician at New York University, and Levent Alpöge of Anthropic, another AI firm, were nearing their own AI-assisted solution. After talks between OpenAI and Dr Buckmaster broke down, the mathematician published his unfinished work with Dr Alpöge online on September 8th, before the announcement from OpenAI (which does not, itself, go into the kind of detail a mathematician normally would about how the solution was arrived at). The two mathematicians’ solution is not complete, but it is close."},{"index":9,"text":"The spat raises questions that will become ever more pertinent as AI is unleashed on humanity’s many unsolved problems. Mathematicians of the human variety had made steady progress on the Navier-Stokes problem over the years, learning much along the way. So, if AI leaps the final hurdle to a solution, who deserves credit? And will the result, as might be expected to happen had humans come to it, open up more areas of research? There is also the troubling question of whether Dr Buckmaster’s and Dr Alpöge’s work, which made use of OpenAI’s products, might have inadvertently snuck into their models’ training data, as Dr Buckmaster suggests may have happened and OpenAI says it cannot rule out."},{"index":10,"text":"To answer one question, OpenAI has said it does not intend to claim the $1m prize. Mathematicians, meanwhile, will be pondering what all of this means for the future of their subject. September 12TH-18TH ｜Science & technology"}]}]}],"reading":[{"file":"9.14 精读(1).pdf","headline":{"title":"Book explores Edna O’Brien’s twilight","titleZh":"","source":"The Guardian","series":"考研那些事儿之文化系列"},"paragraphs":[{"index":1,"en":"After visiting his dying mother in London, Carlo Gébler returned to his rural home in Northern Ireland one night and encountered a hedgehog sitting on the doormat. A thought bubbled up: his mother had died and been reincarnated as this woodland creature.","zh":"在伦敦探望弥留之际的母亲后，卡洛·盖伯勒一天夜里回到他在北爱尔兰的乡下的家中，碰见一只刺猬坐在门垫上。一个念头冒了出来：母亲已经去世，转世成了这只林中生灵。"},{"index":2,"en":"In fact, his mother, the novelist Edna O’Brien, was still alive, but his mind insisted otherwise. “At that moment that’s what I believed. And then of course I stopped believing it because I knew it wasn’t true.” When O’Brien died days later in July 2024, at the age of 93, the literary world mourned the passing of a singular voice who had scandalised her native Ireland with depictions of female sexuality and blazed a trail for Irish writers.","zh":"事实上，他的母亲、小说家埃德娜·奥布莱恩还活着，但他的头脑却执意认为并非如此。“那一刻，我就是这么相信的。后来当然不再相信了，因为我知道那不是真的。”几天后，奥布莱恩于2024年7月去世，享年93岁。文学界哀悼这位独特声音的逝去——她对女性性欲的描写令故乡爱尔兰哗然，也为爱尔兰作家开辟了道路。"},{"index":3,"en":"Now her son has produced a chronicle of O’Brien’s final years, a twilight marked by her struggle to keep working despite illness and frailty, and of the impact of her death on his own life. Published this Friday, No One Tells You: The Last Years of Edna O’Brien documents the writer’s doomed battle to complete a final book about TS Eliot as age and infirmity sapped her powers.","zh":"如今，她的儿子写出了一部编年史，记录奥布莱恩的最后岁月，那是一段尽管疾病缠身、身体虚弱仍努力继续工作的暮年时光，同时也记录了她的去世对他自己生活的影响。本周五出版的《无人告诉你：埃德娜·奥布莱恩的最后岁月》记录了这位作家在年老体衰、精力被消磨之际，为完成一部关于T.S.艾略特的最后著作而进行的一场注定失败的挣扎。"},{"index":4,"en":"O’Brien escaped a stifling and conservative upbringing in County Clare by moving to Dublin and eloping with Ernest Gébler, an Irish-Czech writer, to England, where they raised two sons, Carlo and Sasha, before divorcing. The Country Girls, published in 1960, established her reputation, launching her image as a glamorous public persona in London: a convent-educated rebel who befriended Hollywood stars while writing novels, biographies, plays and poetry.","zh":"奥布莱恩逃离了克莱尔郡令人窒息、保守的成长环境：她搬到都柏林，并与爱尔兰-捷克作家欧内斯特·盖伯勒私奔到英格兰，在那里养育了两个儿子，卡洛和萨沙，后来离婚。1960年出版的《乡村女孩》奠定了她的声誉，塑造了她在伦敦魅力四射的公众形象：一个受过修道院教育的反叛者，在写小说、传记、戏剧和诗歌的同时，还与好莱坞明星交朋友。"},{"index":5,"en":"Female desire and subversion populated her fiction decades before Ireland liberalised and legalised divorce, same-sex marriage and abortion, but that did not entitle Irish people to “co-opt” her memory for political ends, said Gébler. “In Ireland people want a narrative which emphasises the progressive journey, as if she planned all the political progress on the back of an envelope.","zh":"早在在爱尔兰对离婚、同性婚姻和堕胎放宽限制并予以合法化的几十年前，女性欲望与颠覆就已充斥她的小说，但盖伯勒说，这并不赋予爱尔兰人为了政治目的“挪用”她记忆的权利。“在爱尔兰，人们想要一种强调进步历程的叙事，就好像她匆匆策划了所有政治进步一样。"},{"index":6,"en":"Gébler said his mother endured many hostile reviews, initially by critics who deemed her work “unseemly” and later by others who disliked her forays into the lives of historical figures such as James Joyce’s muse and wife, Nora Barnacle, or the kidnapping of girls in Nigeria. “Her fiction changed its register, but the world isn’t very sympathetic to that. It’s kind of: ‘It’s not your gig, madam.’ ”","zh":"盖伯勒说，母亲忍受了许多敌意评论，起初是批评者认为她的作品“不成体统”，后来是另一些人，他们不喜欢她涉足历史人物生活，如乔伊斯的缪斯兼妻子诺拉·巴纳克尔，或是尼日利亚女孩遭绑架事件。“她的小说改变了调性，但世界对此并不太宽容。那意思有点像：‘这不是你该干的事，夫人。’”"},{"index":7,"en":"The posthumous acclaim for his mother validated the morbid joke about death being a great career move, said Gébler. “A living writer is sometimes overlooked because there’s so much other noise, but death is an even bigger noise, so you can get people’s distracted attention.”","zh":"盖伯勒说，母亲去世后获得的赞誉印证了那个病态的玩笑：死亡是一次极好的职业发展策略。“在世的作家有时会被忽视，因为其他噪音太多，但死亡是更大的噪音，于是你就能吸引到人们分散的注意力。”"},{"index":8,"en":"For Gébler, however, her passing brought loss, confusion and dread. “The awful thing about a parent’s death is that you move up to the cliff edge. Once they’re gone, you know you’re next. And then you start to have all sorts of terrible thoughts. Why did I do this to my children? They didn’t ask to be born and then they too are going to die.”","zh":"然而，对盖伯勒来说，她的去世带来了失落、困惑和恐惧。“父母去世的可怕之处在于，你逼近了悬崖边缘。他们一走，你就知道下一个是你。然后你开始产生各种可怕的想法。我为什么要这样对我的孩子？他们并没有要求被生下来，而他们同样也会死去。”"},{"index":9,"en":"The impulse to write the memoir came in her final weeks as he shuttled from his home in County Fermanagh to tend her in London. “The unconscious is very patient and very clever, and it always waits until the right moment. And then comes this very strong sense: right, this is what I must write.”","zh":"写这本回忆录的冲动出现在她生命的最后几周，当时他在弗马纳郡的家与伦敦之间往返奔波，去照料她。“潜意识非常有耐心，也非常聪明，它总是等到合适的时机。然后一种非常强烈的感觉就会浮现：对，这就是我必须写下来的。”"},{"index":10,"en":"The finality of death was unconscionable, said Gébler. “There’s no way around the fact: one’s own extinction is disgusting to contemplate, as is the extinction of other people.”","zh":"盖伯勒说，死亡的终局性令人无法接受。“无法回避的事实是：设想自己的消亡令人厌恶，他人的消亡亦然。”"},{"index":11,"en":"Those close to a dying person had an obligation to let them feel seen and heard, he said. “Human being to human being, you listen. That’s your most important duty.”","zh":"他说，临终者的亲近之人有义务让他们感到自己被看见、被听见。“作为一个人，面对另一个人，你要倾听。那是你最重要的责任。”"},{"index":12,"en":"His other advice is to impose order on the chaos of loss by writing about it, whether in scribbled notes or a published book. “If you just let things fester in the basement of the psyche, they just get bigger and darker and huger and more and more terrible. You have to get it up into the light. And then you can come to some sort of accommodation with it, or a truce.”","zh":"他的另一个建议是，通过书写，来为丧亲之痛带来的混乱建立秩序，无论是潦草笔记还是出版成书。“如果你任由事情在心灵的地下室里溃烂，它们只会变得更大、更黑暗、更臃肿、越来越可怕。你必须把它带到光亮中。然后你才能与它达成某种和解，或休战。”"}],"vocab":[{"term":"twilight","head":"n. /ˈtwaɪlaɪt/ the final stage or period of","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"something; the time after sunset 暮年；黄昏；晚期","exampleZh":"I knew him when he was in the twilight of his career. 我在 他职业生涯的晚期才认识他。[反义词:dawnn.黎明；破晓]","tags":[]},{"term":"rural","head":"adj. /ˈrʊərəl/ relating to the countryside rather than a","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"townorcity乡村的，农村的【️【️考研大纲词汇】","exampleZh":"She prefers rural life to city life. 比起城市生活，她更喜欢乡 村生活。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"encounter","head":"v. /ɪnˈkaʊntə(r)/ to meet or experience","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"something, especially unexpectedly 遇到，遭遇；偶然碰到","exampleZh":"【️【️考研大纲词汇】 We encountered a violent storm on the way home. 我们在 回家途中遭遇了一场猛烈的暴风雨。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"hedgehog","head":"n. /ˈhedʒhɒɡ/ a small brown animal with sharp","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"spines on its back 刺猬","exampleZh":"The hedgehog curled up when we approached. 我们走近 时，刺猬蜷缩了起来。","tags":[]},{"term":"doormat","head":"n. /ˈdɔːmæt/ a mat placed at a door for wiping","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"dirtfromshoes门垫，门口擦鞋垫","exampleZh":"He left his muddy boots on the doormat. 他把沾满泥的靴子 留在了门垫上。","tags":[]},{"term":"bubble","head":"v. /ˈbʌbəl / (~up) (of a feeling or thought) to rise or","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"appeargradually涌起，冒出；起泡【️【️考研大纲词汇】","exampleZh":"A feeling of excitement bubbled up inside him. 一种兴奋感 在他心中涌起。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"reincarnate","head":"v. /ˌriːɪnˈkɑːneɪt/ to be born again in another","definition":"","gloss":"bodyafterdeath使转世；转世投胎","example":"The novel imagines that a dead king could reincarnate as","exampleZh":"acommonfarmer.这部小说想象一位死去的国王可能转世为一 个普通农夫。","tags":[]},{"term":"woodland","head":"n. /ˈwʊdlənd/ land covered with trees 林地，树","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"林","exampleZh":"The children got lost in the dense woodland. 孩子们在茂密 的林地中迷路了。[grasslandn.草原;牧场;草地] 在伦敦探望弥留之际的母亲后，卡洛·盖伯勒一天夜里回到 他在北爱尔兰的乡下的家中，碰见一只刺猬坐在门垫上。一个 念头冒了出来：母亲已经去世，转世成了这只林中生灵。","tags":[]},{"term":"otherwise","head":"adv. /ˈʌðə waɪz/ in a different way or in","definition":"","gloss":"differentcircumstances否则；在其他情况下【️【️考研大纲词","example":"He is usually very patient, but otherwise he can be","exampleZh":"汇】 quitestrict.他通常很有耐心，但在其他情况下也可能相当严 格。[likewiseadv.同样地;也，而且]","tags":[]},{"term":"literary","head":"adj. /ˈlɪtərəri/ relating to literature or books 文学","definition":"","gloss":"的；文学作品的【️【️考研大纲词汇】","example":"The award recognizes outstanding literary achievements","exampleZh":"fromyoungwriters.该奖项表彰年轻作家的杰出文学成就。[形 近：literallyadv.逐字地;照字面地；确实地]","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"mourn","head":"v. /mɔːn/ to feel and express great sadness","definition":"","gloss":"becausesomeonehasdied哀悼；为……的逝世感到悲痛","example":"The whole community gathered to mourn the loss of","exampleZh":"【️【️考研大纲词汇】 thebelovedteacher.整个社区聚集在一起，哀悼那位深受爱戴 的老师的离世。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"singular","head":"adj. /ˈsɪŋɡjələr/ very unusual or special,","definition":"especially in a way that makes someone or something","gloss":"standout独特的；非凡的【️【️考研大纲词汇】","example":"She has a singular talent for making complex ideas easy to","exampleZh":"understand.她有一种非凡的才能，能把复杂的思想讲得很容易 理解。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"scandalise","head":"v. /ˈskændəlaɪz/ to shock or offend people","definition":"because something is considered morally or socially","gloss":"unacceptable（不道德的行为）使震惊，使反感，使愤慨","example":"The politician's careless remarks scandalised many","exampleZh":"membersofthepublic.那位政治人物轻率的言论令许多公众 感到震惊和愤慨。","tags":[]},{"term":"depiction","head":"n. /dɪˈpɪkʃən/ a description or representation","definition":"of someone or something in words, pictures, or","gloss":"otherforms描绘；描述；刻画","example":"The film offers a realistic depiction of life in a small fishing","exampleZh":"village.这部电影真实地描绘了一个小渔村的生活。","tags":[]},{"term":"sexuality","head":"n. /ˌsekʃuˈæləti/ the feelings, behavior, and","definition":"","gloss":"identity related to sexual attraction and relationships 性；","example":"The novel explores how people develop","exampleZh":"性取向；性特征 their understanding of sexuality as they grow older. 这部小 说探讨了人们随着年龄增长如何逐渐认识自己的性特征和性取 向。","tags":[]},{"term":"blaze","head":"v. /bleɪz/ to create a new path or make it easier for","definition":"","gloss":"others to follow by being the first to do something 开辟道","example":"She blazed a trail for women in a field that had long been","exampleZh":"路；闯出新路【️【️考研大纲词汇】 dominatedbymen.她为女性在一个长期由男性主导的领域中 开辟了道路。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"trail","head":"n. /treɪl/ a path or route, especially one that others","definition":"","gloss":"canfollow小路；路线；（比喻）道路【️【️考研大纲词汇】","example":"The scientist's discovery opened a new trail for","exampleZh":"futureresearcherstofollow.这位科学家的发现为未来的研究 人员开辟了一条新的道路。 事实上，他的母亲、小说家埃德娜·奥布莱恩还活着，但他 的头脑却执意认为并非如此。“那一刻，我就是这么相信的。后 来当然不再相信了，因为我知道那不是真的。”几天后，奥布莱 恩于2024年7月去世，享年93岁。文学界哀悼这位独特声音 的逝去——她对女性性欲的描写令故乡爱尔兰哗然，也为爱尔 兰作家开辟了道路。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"chronicle","head":"n. /ˈkrɒnɪkəl/ a written record of events in the","definition":"","gloss":"order in which they happened 编年史，记事录","example":"The documentary serves as a chronicle of the civil rights","exampleZh":"movement.这部纪录片是民权运动的编年记录。","tags":[]},{"term":"frailty","head":"n. /ˈfreɪlti/ the condition of being physically weak","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"and delicate, especially because of old age or illness 虚","exampleZh":"弱，衰弱 Despite her frailty, she continued to care for her garden. 尽 管身体虚弱，她仍继续照料自己的花园。[fragileadj.脆弱的]","tags":[]},{"term":"infirmity","head":"n. /ɪnˈfɜːməti/ physical weakness or illness,","definition":"","gloss":"especiallyinoldage体弱，虚弱；疾病","example":"She refused to let her infirmity stop her from pursuing her","exampleZh":"studies.她不愿让体弱阻止自己继续学业。","tags":[]},{"term":"sap","head":"v. /sæp/ to gradually weaken someone’s strength,","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"energy, or confidence 逐渐削弱，消耗","exampleZh":"Worry can sap your energy over time. 忧虑会逐渐消耗你的 精力。 如今，她的儿子写出了一部编年史，记录奥布莱恩的最后 岁月，那是一段尽管疾病缠身、身体虚弱仍努力继续工作的暮 年时光，同时也记录了她的去世对他自己生活的影响。本周五 出版的《无人告诉你：埃德娜·奥布莱恩的最后岁月》记录了这 位作家在年老体衰、精力被消磨之际，为完成一部关于T.S.艾略 特的最后著作而进行的一场注定失败的挣扎。","tags":[]},{"term":"stifling","head":"adj. /ˈstaɪfəlɪŋ/ preventing free expression or","definition":"","gloss":"development; very hot and airless 令人窒息的；压抑的","example":"The stifling rules at the boarding school made students","exampleZh":"feeltrapped.寄宿学校令人窒息的规则让学生感到被困。","tags":[]},{"term":"conservative","head":"adj. /kənˈsɜːvətɪv/ not liking change or new","definition":"","gloss":"ideas;traditional保守的，守旧的【️【️考研大纲词汇】","example":"Her conservative parents disapproved of her","exampleZh":"unconventionalcareerchoice.她保守的父母不赞成她非传统 的职业选择。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"upbringing","head":"n. /ˈʌpbrɪŋɪŋ/ the way a child is treated and","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"educated while growing up 教养，抚养","exampleZh":"A loving upbringing can help a child feel secure. 充满爱的 教养能帮助孩子感到安全。[bringsomeoneup Phrasalverb养育，抚养;教养]","tags":[]},{"term":"elope","head":"v. /ɪˈləʊp/ to run away secretly with a lover,","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"especially to get married 私奔","exampleZh":"They eloped to Las Vegas without telling anyone. 他们没告 诉任何人就私奔到拉斯维加斯结婚了。","tags":[]},{"term":"reputation","head":"n. /ˌrepjəˈteɪʃən / the belief or opinion that","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"people generally have about someone or something 名","exampleZh":"声，声誉【️【️考研大纲词汇】 The school has a reputation for academic excellence. 这所 学校以学术卓越闻名。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"glamorous","head":"adj. /ˈɡlæmərəs/ attractive and exciting in a","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"way associated with wealth, fame, or fashion 迷人的，光鲜","exampleZh":"的 The actress looked glamorous in her evening gown. 那位女 演员穿着晚礼服，光彩照人。","tags":[]},{"term":"persona","head":"n. /pəˈsəʊnə/ the image or character that","definition":"","gloss":"someone presents to other people 形象，人格面具","example":"The politician’s friendly persona hid his ruthless","exampleZh":"ambition.那位政客友好的形象掩盖了他冷酷的野心。","tags":[]},{"term":"convent","head":"n. /ˈkɒnvənt/ a place where nuns live and work,","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"oraschoolrunbynuns女修道院；修道院学校","exampleZh":"She was educated at a convent in the countryside. 她在乡 间的一所修道院学校接受教育。","tags":[]},{"term":"rebel","head":"n. /ˈrebəl/ a person who refuses to obey authority or","definition":"","gloss":"followacceptedrules反叛者，叛逆者【️【️考研大纲词汇】","example":"As a teenager, he was a rebel who questioned everything.","exampleZh":"青少年时，他是个质疑一切的反叛者。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"befriend","head":"v. /bɪˈfrend/ to become friendly with someone,","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"especially to help or support them 与…交朋友；帮助","exampleZh":"A kind neighbor befriended the lonely old man. 一位善良的 邻居与那位孤独的老人交朋友。","tags":[]},{"term":"biography","head":"n. /baɪˈɒɡrəfi/ a book or account of someone’s","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"life written by another person 传记【️【️考研大纲词汇】","exampleZh":"She read a biography of a famous artist. 她读了一本著名艺 术家的传记。 奥布莱恩逃离了克莱尔郡令人窒息、保守的成长环境：她 搬到都柏林，并与爱尔兰-捷克作家欧内斯特·盖伯勒私奔到英格 兰，在那里养育了两个儿子，卡洛和萨沙，后来离婚。1960年 出版的《乡村女孩》奠定了她的声誉，塑造了她在伦敦魅力四 射的公众形象：一个受过修道院教育的反叛者，在写小说、传 记、戏剧和诗歌的同时，还与好莱坞明星交朋友。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"populate","head":"v. /ˈpɒpjəleɪt/ to fill or be present in large","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"numbers throughout a place or thing 充斥，充满；居住于","exampleZh":"Tourists populate the beaches every summer. 每年夏天游客 挤满这些海滩。","tags":[]},{"term":"progressive","head":"adj. /prəˈɡresɪv/ favoring new ideas, social","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"change, or reform; moving forward 进步的，先进的；渐进的","exampleZh":"【️【️考研大纲词汇】 She is known for her progressive views on education. 她以 在教育方面的进步观点而闻名。 早在在爱尔兰对离婚、同性婚姻和堕胎放宽限制并予以合 法化的几十年前，女性欲望与颠覆就已充斥她的小说，但盖伯 勒说，这并不赋予爱尔兰人为了政治目的“挪用”她记忆的权 利。“在爱尔兰，人们想要一种强调进步历程的叙事，就好像她 匆匆策划了所有政治进步一样。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"endure","head":"v. /ɪnˈdjʊə(r)/ to suffer something painful or","definition":"","gloss":"difficultpatiently忍受，忍耐【️【️考研大纲词汇】","example":"She endured years of criticism before her talent was","exampleZh":"recognized.她忍受了多年的批评，直到她的才华得到认可。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"hostile","head":"adj. /ˈhɒstaɪl/ showing strong dislike or opposition","definition":"","gloss":"敌意的，不友好的【️【️考研大纲词汇】","example":"The proposal met with a hostile reception from local","exampleZh":"residents.该提议遭到当地居民的敌意对待。[形近反义： hospitableadj.好客的、热情待人的]","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"unseemly","head":"adj. /ʌnˈsiːmli/ not proper, polite, or suitable 不","definition":"","gloss":"体面的，不得体的","example":"His unseemly behavior at the dinner embarrassed","exampleZh":"everyone.他在晚宴上不得体的行为让所有人难堪。","tags":[]},{"term":"foray","head":"n. /ˈfɒreɪ/ a short attempt to become involved in a","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"new area or activity 涉足，尝试","exampleZh":"Her first foray into politics ended in defeat. 她首次涉足政治 以失败告终。","tags":[]},{"term":"register","head":"n. /ˈredʒɪstə(r)/ a particular range, style, or level","definition":"","gloss":"oflanguageorexpression语域，语体风格；调性","example":"People chatting at a party will usually be talking in (an)","exampleZh":"informalregister.人们在聚会上聊天通常都是用非正式语体。","tags":[]},{"term":"sympathetic","head":"adj. /ˌsɪmpəˈθetɪk/ showing agreement,","definition":"","gloss":"approval,orsupport赞同的，支持的；同情的【️【️考研大纲词","example":"The committee was sympathetic to our request for","exampleZh":"汇】 funding.委员会赞同我们的资金申请。","tags":[]},{"term":"gig","head":"n. /ɡɪɡ/ a job, role, or task, especially one that suits a","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"person工作，差事","exampleZh":"Teaching online turned out to be a perfect gig for her. 在线 教学对她来说是一份完美的工作。 盖伯勒说，母亲忍受了许多敌意评论，起初是批评者认为 她的作品“不成体统”，后来是另一些人，他们不喜欢她涉足历 史人物生活，如乔伊斯的缪斯兼妻子诺拉·巴纳克尔，或是尼日 利亚女孩遭绑架事件。“她的小说改变了调性，但世界对此并不 太宽容。那意思有点像：‘这不是你该干的事，夫人。’”","tags":[]},{"term":"posthumous","head":"adj. /ˈpɒstʃəməs/ happening or given after","definition":"","gloss":"someone’sdeath死后的，身后的","example":"The artist received posthumous recognition when a","exampleZh":"gallery exhibited his paintings after his death. 这位艺术家去 世后，一家画廊展出了他的画作，使他获得了身后的认可。","tags":[]},{"term":"acclaim","head":"n. /əˈkleɪm/ public praise and approval 赞誉，喝彩","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"【️【️考研大纲词汇】","exampleZh":"Her novel won critical acclaim around the world. 她的小说 赢得了世界各地评论界的赞誉。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"validate","head":"v. /ˈvælɪdeɪt/ to prove that something is true or","definition":"","gloss":"correct证实，确认","example":"The experiment’s results validated the scientist’s","exampleZh":"originaltheory.实验结果证实了这位科学家最初的理论。","tags":[]},{"term":"morbid","head":"adj. /ˈmɔːbɪd/ (disapproving) too interested in","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"unpleasant subjects, especially death（尤指对死亡等）病态","exampleZh":"的，着迷的 He has a morbid fascination with death and disaster. 他对 死亡和灾难有一种病态的兴趣。","tags":[]},{"term":"overlook","head":"v. /ˌəʊvəˈlʊk/ to fail to notice or give attention to","definition":"","gloss":"something忽视，忽略【️【️考研大纲词汇】","example":"The manager overlooked her contribution and promoted","exampleZh":"someoneelse.经理忽视了她的贡献，提拔了别人。 盖伯勒说，母亲去世后获得的赞誉印证了那个病态的玩笑： 死亡是一次极好的职业发展策略。“在世的作家有时会被忽视， 因为其他噪音太多，但死亡是更大的噪音，于是你就能吸引到 人们分散的注意力。”","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"confusion","head":"n. /kənˈfjuːʒən/ a state of not understanding","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"whatishappeningorwhattodo困惑，迷惑；混乱【️【️考研","exampleZh":"大纲词汇】 There was widespread confusion about the new rules. 人们 对于新规定普遍感到困惑。","tags":[]},{"term":"dread","head":"n. /dred/ a feeling of great fear or worry about","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"somethingthatmayhappen恐惧，惧怕；担忧【️【️考研大纲","exampleZh":"词汇】 He felt a deep dread before the operation. 手术前他感到深 深的恐惧。","tags":[]},{"term":"cliff","head":"n. /klɪf/ a high steep rock face, especially at the edge","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"oftheseaoramountain悬崖，峭壁【️【️考研大纲词汇】","exampleZh":"They walked carefully along the cliff path. 他们沿着悬崖小 路小心行走。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"edge","head":"n. /edʒ/ the outer or furthest point of something,","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"especially where it ends or meets another surface 边缘，边","exampleZh":"沿【️【️考研大纲词汇】 She sat on the edge of the bed. 她坐在床边。 然而，对盖伯勒来说，她的去世带来了失落、困惑和恐惧。 “父母去世的可怕之处在于，你逼近了悬崖边缘。他们一走， 你就知道下一个是你。然后你开始产生各种可怕的想法。我为 什么要这样对我的孩子？他们并没有要求被生下来，而他们同 样也会死去。”","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"impulse","head":"n. /ˈɪmpʌls/ a sudden strong urge or desire to do","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"something冲动；突然的念头【️【️考研大纲词汇】","exampleZh":"She bought the expensive dress on impulse. 她一时冲动买 下了那条昂贵的连衣裙。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"memoir","head":"n. /ˈmemwɑːr/ a book or other piece of writing","definition":"based on the writer's personal knowledge of famous","gloss":"people, places, or events 传记，传略","example":"The former soldier published a memoir about his years at","exampleZh":"war.那位前士兵出版了一本关于他战争岁月的传记。","tags":[]},{"term":"shuttle","head":"v. /ˈʃʌtəl/ to travel regularly between two places,","definition":"","gloss":"especiallyoverashortdistance往返穿梭，频繁往返【️【️考研大","example":"He shuttles between Shanghai and New York for work every","exampleZh":"纲词汇】 week.他每周往返于上海和纽约之间工作。 写这本回忆录的冲动出现在她生命的最后几周，当时他在 弗马纳郡的家与伦敦之间往返奔波，去照料她。“潜意识非常有 耐心，也非常聪明，它总是等到合适的时机。然后一种非常强 烈的感觉就会浮现：对，这就是我必须写下来的。”","tags":[]},{"term":"unconscionable","head":"adj. /ʌnˈkɒnʃənəbəl/ not right or","definition":"","gloss":"reasonable; morally unacceptable 道德上不可接受的","example":"To make people feel shame or guilt for being ill is","exampleZh":"unconscionable.让人们因为生病而感到羞耻和负疚是不道德 的。[conscience n. 良心]","tags":[]},{"term":"extinction","head":"n. /ɪkˈstɪŋkʃn/ the state of no longer existing or","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"being completely destroyed 灭绝，消亡","exampleZh":"Many birds are threatened with extinction. 许多鸟类面临灭 绝威胁。","tags":[]},{"term":"contemplate","head":"v. /ˈkɒntəmpleɪt/ to think about something","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"seriouslyandforalongtime沉思，考虑；思量【️【️考研大纲","exampleZh":"词汇】 He contemplated moving to another country. 他考虑过搬到 另一个国家。 盖伯勒说，死亡的终局性令人无法接受。“无法回避的事实 是：设想自己的消亡令人厌恶，他人的消亡亦然。”","tags":[]},{"term":"obligation","head":"n. /ˌɒblɪˈɡeɪʃən/ a moral or legal requirement","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"thatyoumustfulfil义务，责任【️【️考研大纲词汇】","exampleZh":"Parents have an obligation to provide for their children. 父 母有义务抚养孩子。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"duty","head":"n. /ˈdjuːti/ something you must do because it is","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"morally or legally right; a responsibility 责任，义务，职责","exampleZh":"It is your duty to report the accident to the police. 你有责 任向警方报告这起事故。 他说，临终者的亲近之人有义务让他们感到自己被看见、 被听见。“作为一个人，面对另一个人，你要倾听。那是你最重 要的责任。”","tags":[]},{"term":"scribble","head":"v. /ˈskrɪbəl/ to write or draw something quickly or","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"carelessly匆匆地写；乱涂乱画","exampleZh":"The baby's just scribbled all over my new dictionary！这个 孩子刚才在我的新词典上乱涂乱画！","tags":[]},{"term":"fester","head":"v. /ˈfestər/ to become worse, more bitter, or more","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"painful over time, especially because of being ignored 溃","exampleZh":"烂；恶化；郁积 Resentment can fester if it is never discussed. 怨恨如果从不 沟通就会不断郁积。","tags":[]},{"term":"basement","head":"n. /ˈbeɪsmənt/ a part of a building that is below","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"groundlevel地下室【️【️考研大纲词汇】","exampleZh":"The old boxes were left in the basement for years. 那些旧 箱子在地下室放了好多年。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"psyche","head":"n. /ˈsaɪki/ the mind, soul, or spirit of a person 心","definition":"","gloss":"","example":"灵，心理，精神","exampleZh":"Music can have a powerful effect on the human psyche. 音 乐能对人的心灵产生强大影响。","tags":[]},{"term":"accommodation","head":"n. /əˌkɒməˈdeɪʃən/ a compromise or","definition":"agreement that allows people to deal with a difficult","gloss":"situation和解，妥协；适应【️【️考研大纲词汇】","example":"They reached an accommodation that satisfied both sides.","exampleZh":"他们达成了一项令双方都满意的妥协。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"truce","head":"n. /truːs/ an agreement between enemies or","definition":"","gloss":"opponents to stop fighting or arguing for a certain time 停","example":"The arguing couple called a truce for the sake of the 8 September 2026 | The Guardian","exampleZh":"战，休战；暂时和解 children.那对争吵的夫妇为了孩子暂时休战。[truthn.真相] 他的另一个建议是，通过书写，来为丧亲之痛带来的混乱 建立秩序，无论是潦草笔记还是出版成书。“如果你任由事情在 心灵的地下室里溃烂，它们只会变得更大、更黑暗、更臃肿、 越来越可怕。你必须把它带到光亮中。然后你才能与它达成某 种和解，或休战。” 群公告学习流程精读，","tags":[]}]}],"tests":[{"file":"【福利】9.14 检验题__阅读理解(1).docx","type":"reading","instructions":[],"passage":[],"items":[{"number":21,"stem":"The author mentions the hedgehog episode in Paragraph 1 in order to _____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"prove that Carlo Gébler continued to believe in reincarnation after his mother's death"},{"key":"B","text":"introduce a moment in which grief temporarily distorted Gébler's perception of reality"},{"key":"C","text":"show that Edna O'Brien had asked her son to memorialise her through a woodland creature"},{"key":"D","text":"explain why Gébler's memoir concentrates on TS Eliot rather than on family relationships"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"[B]","analysis":["【题型】例证题·开篇案例功能","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraphs 1-2","【解析】","- 原文（Para.1）：After visiting his dying mother in London, Carlo Gébler returned to his rural home in Northern Ireland one night and encountered a hedgehog sitting on the doormat. A thought bubbled up: his mother had died and been reincarnated as this woodland creature.","- 原文（Para.2）：In fact, his mother, the novelist Edna O’Brien, was still alive, but his mind insisted otherwise. “At that moment that’s what I believed. And then of course I stopped believing it because I knew it wasn’t true.”","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：时态与程度陷阱。Gébler 说 At that moment 相信，随后 stopped believing，说明只是短暂想法，不是持续相信。","- 选项B（正确）：母亲实际仍在世，但他的 mind insisted otherwise。刺猬场景引入了临终压力下一个短暂而非理性的认知瞬间。","- 选项C（错误）：无中生有陷阱。原文没有说 O'Brien 提出过任何这类纪念要求。","- 选项D（错误）：细节杂糅陷阱。TS Eliot 出现在第 3 段 O'Brien 未完成的最后著作中，与开篇刺猬例子的功能无关。","【命题技巧】","- 例证与下文转折：Para.2 的 In fact 揭示开篇场景的性质是主观误认。","- 时间限定识别：At that moment 与 stopped believing 限定信念的短暂性。"]},{"number":22,"stem":"According to Paragraph 3, No One Tells You records _____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"O’Brien’s escape from County Clare and the publication of The Country Girls"},{"key":"B","text":"O’Brien’s friendships with Hollywood stars and her early public image in London"},{"key":"C","text":"the hostile reviews that met O’Brien’s fiction about Nora Barnacle and Nigeria"},{"key":"D","text":"O’Brien’s final struggle with illness and the effect of her death on her son"}],"answer":"D","kicker":"[D]","analysis":["【题型】细节理解题·著作内容识别","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 3","【解析】","- 原文（Para.3）：Now her son has produced a chronicle of O’Brien’s final years, a twilight marked by her struggle to keep working despite illness and frailty, and of the impact of her death on his own life.","- 原文（Para.3）：No One Tells You: The Last Years of Edna O’Brien documents the writer’s doomed battle to complete a final book about TS Eliot as age and infirmity sapped her powers.","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：段落错位陷阱。逃离保守成长环境和《The Country Girls》出版属于第 4 段的生平回顾。","- 选项B（错误）：段落错位陷阱。好莱坞明星友谊和 glamorous public persona 同样属于第 4 段。","- 选项C（错误）：段落错位陷阱。敌意书评和 Nora Barnacle、Nigeria 等题材在第 6 段讨论。","- 选项D（正确）：第 3 段明确说书记录 O'Brien 在疾病衰弱中坚持写作的最后岁月，以及她的死亡对儿子生活的影响。","【命题技巧】","- 著作主题主干定位：chronicle、final years、impact of her death 是三个核心信息点。","- 前后段落内容排除：早期经历、书评和公众形象都不能替代最后岁月这一主题。"]},{"number":23,"stem":"The word “co-opt” (Paragraph 5) is closest in meaning to _____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"take over something and use it for a particular purpose"},{"key":"B","text":"formally honour a writer whose work changed national culture"},{"key":"C","text":"remove a writer from the historical record of her own country"},{"key":"D","text":"translate a writer's private fiction into a political manifesto"}],"answer":"A","kicker":"[A]","analysis":["【题型】词义猜测题·动词语境理解","【难度】⭐⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 5","【解析】","- 原文（Para.5）：Female desire and subversion populated her fiction decades before Ireland liberalised and legalised divorce, same-sex marriage and abortion, but that did not entitle Irish people to “co-opt” her memory for political ends, said Gébler.","- 原文（Para.5）：In Ireland people want a narrative which emphasises the progressive journey, as if she planned all the political progress on the back of an envelope.","分析：","- 选项A（正确）：Gébler 反对把 O'Brien 的记忆用于 political ends；下文说爱尔兰人想把她纳入进步叙事，仿佛政治进步由她规划。co-opt 即“挪用、收编”。","- 选项B（错误）：情感方向偏差陷阱。该词带有“未经授权挪用”的批评意味，不是正式致敬。","- 选项C（错误）：程度过度陷阱。原文批评政治化使用她的记忆，没有说从历史记录中删除她。","- 选项D（错误）：细节偷换陷阱。政治化叙事不等于把小说直接翻译成政治宣言。","【命题技巧】","- 引号词的情感色彩：co-opt 加引号并搭配 political ends，提示批判性含义。","- 下文解释法：progressive journey 与 planned all the political progress 具体说明挪用方式。"]},{"number":24,"stem":"Carlo Gébler’s remark about death being “a great career move” implies that _____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"a living writer’s reputation normally declines as ordinary literary noise increases"},{"key":"B","text":"the literary world deliberately ignores dead writers until their books become rare"},{"key":"C","text":"posthumous attention can make a writer more visible than she was amid ordinary distractions"},{"key":"D","text":"critical recognition after death matters more than the quality of the writer’s work"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"[C]","analysis":["【题型】人物观点题·句意推理","【难度】⭐⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 7","【解析】","- 原文（Para.7）：The posthumous acclaim for his mother validated the morbid joke about death being a great career move, said Gébler. “A living writer is sometimes overlooked because there’s so much other noise, but death is an even bigger noise, so you can get people’s distracted attention.”","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：因果偷换陷阱。原文说在世作家 sometimes overlooked 是因为外部噪音增加，不是声誉通常随噪音下降。","- 选项B（错误）：无中生有陷阱。原文没有提书籍稀有或文学界故意忽视已故作家。","- 选项C（正确）：他把 death 称作 even bigger noise，能获得 people’s distracted attention；母亲死后受到 posthumous acclaim 正印证这一说法。","- 选项D（错误）：价值过度推理陷阱。他在谈注意力机制，没有比较死后名声与作品质量的重要性。","【命题技巧】","- 反讽句解码：morbid joke 与 career move 的组合指向死后关注度上升。","- 对比结构定位：living writer overlooked 与 death is an even bigger noise 构成对比。"]},{"number":25,"stem":"Which of the following would be the best title for the text?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Death as a Career Move: The Making of Edna O’Brien’s Fame"},{"key":"B","text":"Final Years in the Light: A Son’s Memoir of Edna O’Brien"},{"key":"C","text":"Rebel from Clare: How Edna O’Brien Planned Ireland’s Liberal Journey"},{"key":"D","text":"Writing About TS Eliot: Edna O’Brien’s Last and Greatest Book"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"[B]","analysis":["【题型】主旨大意题·最佳标题","【难度】⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Full text","【解析】","- 原文（Para.3）：Now her son has produced a chronicle of O’Brien’s final years... and of the impact of her death on his own life.","- 原文（Para.9）：The impulse to write the memoir came in her final weeks as he shuttled from his home in County Fermanagh to tend her in London.","- 原文（Para.12）：“...writing about it, whether in scribbled notes or a published book... You have to get it up into the light. And then you can come to some sort of accommodation with it, or a truce.”","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：以偏概全陷阱。第 7 段谈死后名声，但全文中心是儿子如何书写母亲最后岁月与自身哀伤。","- 选项B（正确）：Final years 对应 memoir 的核心范围，son’s memoir 点明作者关系，light 呼应文末通过写作处理失序哀伤的建议。","- 选项C（错误）：反向主旨陷阱。Gébler 明确反对把母亲记忆说成爱尔兰进步历程的预先规划。","- 选项D（错误）：细节扩大陷阱。TS Eliot 书是 O'Brien 未完成的最后著作，不是全文主线，也不是已完成的伟大作品。","【命题技巧】","- 文章体裁判断：访谈式书评的主线是新 memoir 的内容与写作动机。","- 首尾呼应整合：开篇临终场景与文末写作疗愈共同限定标题范围。","— — — — — — — — — —","本文档由我们自研的考研英语阅读理解大模型生成。该模型基于近10年考研英语真题进行训练。我们会根据同学们的反馈持续优化模型能力。","本文档为【福利】，不提供答疑服务，欢迎大家自行交流使用。","【两个常见问题】","随着AI技术的不断进步，通过知识投喂和持续训练，大模型已逐渐具备模仿真题命题风格的能力。这份文档正是这一尝试的成果。它仅作为福利提供给大家，起到辅助检验的作用。你可以根据自身需要选择使用，如果有所顾虑，完全可以不用。","如果对答案解析有疑惑，直接打开任何AI大模型（如Deepseek、Kimi等），上传该篇原文文档，然后复制粘贴对应的题目和选项，说清楚你的疑问，大模型会回应解释对应的问题。"]}]},{"file":"【福利】9.14检验题__完型填空(1).docx","type":"cloze","instructions":["Directions: Read the following text. Choose the best word(s) for each numbered blank and mark A, B, C or D on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points)"],"passage":["The posthumous acclaim for his mother validated the morbid joke about death being a great career move, said Gébler. “A living writer is sometimes ____【11】____ because there’s so much other noise, but death is an even bigger noise, so you can get people’s ____【12】____ attention.”","For Gébler, however, her passing brought loss, ____【13】____ and dread. “The awful thing about a parent’s death is that you move up to the cliff ____【14】____. Once they’re gone, you know you’re ____【15】____. And then you start to have all sorts of terrible thoughts. Why did I do this to my children? They didn’t ask to be ____【16】____ and then they too are going to die.”","The ____【17】____ to write the memoir came in her final weeks as he ____【18】____ from his home in County Fermanagh to tend her in London. “The unconscious is very ____【19】____ and very clever, and it always ____【20】____ until the right moment. And then comes this very strong sense: right, this is what I must write.”"],"items":[{"number":11,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"rewarded"},{"key":"B","text":"overlooked"},{"key":"C","text":"protected"},{"key":"D","text":"remembered"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"B (overlooked)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·语境语义","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 1, 第2句","【解析】","[A] rewarded（奖励）： living writer 被其他声音淹没，不是被奖励。逻辑方向干扰。","[B] overlooked（被忽视）：因为噪声太多，在世作家有时被忽视。正确答案。","[C] protected（被保护）：与“其他噪声”造成的遮蔽效果无关。语义场干扰。","[D] remembered（被记得）：死亡才带来更大关注，说明在世时相对被忽略。近义干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":12,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"divided"},{"key":"B","text":"absent"},{"key":"C","text":"distracted"},{"key":"D","text":"reluctant"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"C (distracted)","analysis":["【题型】形容词辨析·注意力语义","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 1, 第2句","【解析】","[A] divided（分散的）：可修饰 attention，但不能体现死亡把注意力强行吸走。程度差异干扰。","[B] absent（缺席的）：注意力并非完全不存在。程度差异干扰。","[C] distracted（被带偏的、分散的）：distracted attention 呼应 death is an even bigger noise。正确答案。","[D] reluctant（不情愿的）：attention 本身不具意愿。搭配干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":13,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"certainty"},{"key":"B","text":"comfort"},{"key":"C","text":"relief"},{"key":"D","text":"confusion"}],"answer":"D","kicker":"D (confusion)","analysis":["【题型】名词辨析·情感语义","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2, 第1句","【解析】","[A] certainty（确定）：与面对父母去世的心理冲击不符。逻辑方向干扰。","[B] comfort（安慰）：方向相反。逻辑方向干扰。","[C] relief（解脱）：感情色彩不合。逻辑方向干扰。","[D] confusion（困惑）：与 loss、dread 并列，表现失去、困惑和恐惧。正确答案。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":14,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"summit"},{"key":"B","text":"face"},{"key":"C","text":"edge"},{"key":"D","text":"boundary"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"C (edge)","analysis":["【题型】名词辨析·隐喻搭配","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2, 第2句","【解析】","[A] summit（顶点）：不构成 cliff summit 的常见隐喻。搭配干扰。","[B] face（面）：cliff face 指崖壁，但 move up to the cliff edge 才表示逼近危险边界。近义干扰。","[C] edge（边缘）：cliff edge 比喻死亡临近，自己“排到了下一步”。正确答案。","[D] boundary（边界）：语义泛化，缺少悬崖边缘的具体意象。近义干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":15,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"next"},{"key":"B","text":"free"},{"key":"C","text":"safe"},{"key":"D","text":"last"}],"answer":"A","kicker":"A (next)","analysis":["【题型】形容词/代词辨析·序列语义","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2, 第2句","【解析】","[A] next（下一个）：Once they’re gone, you know you’re next 指父母去世后自己成为下一个。正确答案。","[B] free（自由的）：与死亡临近的语境相反。逻辑方向干扰。","[C] safe（安全的）：方向相反。逻辑方向干扰。","[D] last（最后的）：不是排序为最后，而是下一个面对死亡。近义干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":16,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"blamed"},{"key":"B","text":"praised"},{"key":"C","text":"forgotten"},{"key":"D","text":"born"}],"answer":"D","kicker":"D (born)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·生命语义","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2, 第4句","【解析】","[A] blamed（被责备）：句子讨论孩子未经同意来到世间。语义场干扰。","[B] praised（被表扬）：与 ask to be 的被动处境无关。语义场干扰。","[C] forgotten（被遗忘）：不是被遗忘的问题。语义场干扰。","[D] born（出生）：didn’t ask to be born 指孩子没有要求被生下来。正确答案。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":17,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"refusal"},{"key":"B","text":"impulse"},{"key":"C","text":"obligation"},{"key":"D","text":"ability"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"B (impulse)","analysis":["【题型】名词辨析·动机语义","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 3, 第1句","【解析】","[A] refusal（拒绝）：与最终写下回忆录矛盾。逻辑方向干扰。","[B] impulse（冲动）：the impulse to write 指写作冲动。正确答案。","[C] obligation（义务）：后文强调内在意识等到合适时机，不是外部义务。语义场干扰。","[D] ability（能力）：能力不解释为何在最后几周产生写作行动。近义干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":18,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"escaped"},{"key":"B","text":"retired"},{"key":"C","text":"shuttled"},{"key":"D","text":"migrated"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"C (shuttled)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·往返运动","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 3, 第1句","【解析】","[A] escaped（逃离）：他不是逃离家，而是往返照顾母亲。逻辑方向干扰。","[B] retired（退休）：与通勤照护动作无关。语义场干扰。","[C] shuttled（往返穿梭）：shuttled from his home... to tend her 表示两地往返。正确答案。","[D] migrated（迁移）：指较长期移居，不表示反复往返。程度差异干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":19,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"patient"},{"key":"B","text":"noisy"},{"key":"C","text":"silent"},{"key":"D","text":"restless"}],"answer":"A","kicker":"A (patient)","analysis":["【题型】形容词辨析·性格隐喻","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 3, 第2句","【解析】","[A] patient（有耐心的）：patient and very clever 指潜意识会等待合适时机。正确答案。","[B] noisy（吵闹的）：与 waits until the right moment 矛盾。逻辑方向干扰。","[C] silent（沉默的）：沉默不能说明它会主动等待时机。近义干扰。","[D] restless（躁动的）：与耐心等待相反。逻辑方向干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":20,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"insists"},{"key":"B","text":"hesitates"},{"key":"C","text":"disappears"},{"key":"D","text":"waits"}],"answer":"D","kicker":"D (waits)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·时间关系","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 3, 第2句","【解析】","[A] insists（坚持）：insist 不及物使用时后续 on，语法和语义均不合。搭配干扰。","[B] hesitates（犹豫）：犹豫带有不确定，而非有策略地等待。近义干扰。","[C] disappears（消失）：与 right moment 到来时出现矛盾。逻辑方向干扰。","[D] waits（等待）：waits until the right moment 指等到合适时机。正确答案。","— — — — — — — — — —","本文档由我们自研的考研英语完型填空大模型生成。该模型基于近10年考研英语真题进行训练。我们会根据同学们的反馈持续优化模型能力。","本文档为【福利】，不提供答疑服务，欢迎大家自行交流使用。","【两个常见问题】","随着AI技术的不断进步，通过知识投喂和持续训练，大模型已逐渐具备模仿真题命题风格的能力。这份文档正是这一尝试的成果。它仅作为福利提供给大家，起到辅助检验的作用。你可以根据自身需要选择使用，如果有所顾虑，完全可以不用。"]}]},{"file":"【福利】9.14检验题__新题型__段落排序(1).docx","type":"ordering","instructions":["Directions:"],"passage":["Part B","The following paragraphs are given in a wrong order. For Questions 41-45, you are required to reorganize these paragraphs into a coherent text by choosing from the list A-G and filling them into the numbered boxes. Paragraphs B and D have been correctly placed. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points)","[A] Gébler said his mother endured many hostile reviews, initially by critics who deemed her work “unseemly” and later by others who disliked her forays into the lives of historical figures such as James Joyce’s muse and wife, Nora Barnacle, or the kidnapping of girls in Nigeria. “Her fiction changed its register, but the world isn’t very sympathetic to that. It’s kind of: ‘It’s not your gig, madam.’”","[B] In fact, his mother, the novelist Edna O’Brien, was still alive, but his mind insisted otherwise. “At that moment that’s what I believed. And then of course I stopped believing it because I knew it wasn’t true.” When O’Brien died days later in July 2024, at the age of 93, the literary world mourned the passing of a singular voice who had scandalised her native Ireland with depictions of female sexuality and blazed a trail for Irish writers.","[C] Female desire and subversion populated her fiction decades before Ireland liberalised and legalised divorce, same-sex marriage and abortion, but that did not entitle Irish people to “co-opt” her memory for political ends, said Gébler. “In Ireland people want a narrative which emphasises the progressive journey, as if she planned all the political progress on the back of an envelope.","[D] For Gébler, however, her passing brought loss, confusion and dread. “The awful thing about a parent’s death is that you move up to the cliff edge. Once they’re gone, you know you’re next. And then you start to have all sorts of terrible thoughts. Why did I do this to my children? They didn’t ask to be born and then they too are going to die.”","[E] The posthumous acclaim for his mother validated the morbid joke about death being a great career move, said Gébler. “A living writer is sometimes overlooked because there’s so much other noise, but death is an even bigger noise, so you can get people’s distracted attention.”","[F] O’Brien escaped a stifling and conservative upbringing in County Clare by moving to Dublin and eloping with Ernest Gébler, an Irish-Czech writer, to England, where they raised two sons, Carlo and Sasha, before divorcing. The Country Girls, published in 1960, established her reputation, launching her image as a glamorous public persona in London: a convent-educated rebel who befriended Hollywood stars while writing novels, biographies, plays and poetry.","[G] Now her son has produced a chronicle of O’Brien’s final years, a twilight marked by her struggle to keep working despite illness and frailty, and of the impact of her death on his own life. Published this Friday, No One Tells You: The Last Years of Edna O’Brien documents the writer’s doomed battle to complete a final book about TS Eliot as age and infirmity sapped her powers.","B | 41 | 42 | 43 | 44 | 45 | D"],"items":[{"number":41,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"G","kicker":"【答案】G","analysis":["【定位】B段末句与空位41；空位42前。","【解析】B段已经交代 O’Brien 去世和文学界悼念，【G】首句“Now her son has produced a chronicle…”中的“Now”承接死亡事件，“her son”回指前文人物关系，并引出关于其最后岁月的新书。这样，文章由“作家去世”进入“儿子记录并反思母亲最后阶段”的中心话题。","逐项分析"]},{"number":42,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"F","kicker":"【答案】F","analysis":["【定位】空位42；空位43前。","【解析】【F】介绍 O’Brien 从 County Clare 出走、移居都柏林和英格兰，以及 The Country Girls 使其成名。这一段把 G段中的“作家”扩展为完整的成长与声誉背景，也为后文讨论其女性欲望、社会反叛和评论争议提供基础。","逐项分析"]},{"number":43,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"C","kicker":"【答案】C","analysis":["【定位】空位43；空位44前。","【解析】【C】集中讨论“female desire and subversion”，并把它与爱尔兰后来在离婚、同性婚姻、堕胎等问题上的变化相对照。它承接【F】的 The Country Girls 和作家形象，继续解释其文学内容与社会意义，也为【A】中“hostile reviews”的争议提供原因。","逐项分析"]},{"number":44,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"A","kicker":"【答案】A","analysis":["【定位】空位44；空位45前。","【解析】【A】用“initially”和“later”梳理母亲遭受敌意评论的两个阶段，直接承接【C】中大胆、越界的创作主题。【A】末尾“It’s not your gig, madam”也强化了外界对她进入不同题材的不满，为【E】中去世后的赞誉制造转折。","逐项分析"]},{"number":45,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"E","kicker":"【答案】E","analysis":["【定位】空位45；固定段D之前。","【解析】【E】中的“The posthumous acclaim”与【A】的“hostile reviews”形成对照：生前遭遇批评，去世后却获得更高关注。段中“death is an even bigger noise”既承接 O’Brien 的死亡，也自然过渡到【D】“For Gébler, however, her passing brought loss…”。","逐项分析","干扰项设置","本题主要使用时间错位、评价方向干扰和回指错位。【A】【C】【E】都涉及 O’Brien 的文学意义与社会接受，但侧重点不同；【F】与【G】都可用作背景信息，需要通过“Now her son…”这一强衔接词判断全文由死讯进入新作。","— — — — — — — — — —","本文档由我们自研的考研英语新题型大模型生成。该模型基于近10年考研英语真题进行训练。我们会根据同学们的反馈持续优化模型能力。目前是在测试阶段，该题型仅提供到12月底。","本文档为【福利】，不提供答疑服务，欢迎大家自行交流使用。","【两个常见问题】","随着AI技术的不断进步，通过知识投喂和持续训练，大模型已逐渐具备模仿真题命题风格的能力。这份文档正是这一尝试的成果。它仅作为福利提供给大家，起到辅助检验的作用。你可以根据自身需要选择使用，如果有所顾虑，完全可以不用。"]}]}],"qa":[],"originals":[{"kind":"source","label":"原文","file":"9.14—9.20 原文(1).docx","ext":".docx","bytes":155530,"date":"2026-09-14","range":"2026-09-14|2026-09-20","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\9.14—9.20 原文(1).docx","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"source","label":"原文","file":"9.14—9.20 原文(1).pdf","ext":".pdf","bytes":395414,"date":"2026-09-14","range":"2026-09-14|2026-09-20","title":"Book explores Edna O’Brien’s twilight","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\9.14—9.20 原文(1).pdf","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"reading","label":"精读","file":"9.14 精读(1).pdf","ext":".pdf","bytes":869843,"date":"2026-09-14","range":"","title":"Book explores Edna O’Brien’s twilight","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\9.14 精读(1).pdf","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"test","label":"检验题","file":"【福利】9.14 检验题__阅读理解(1).docx","ext":".docx","bytes":57414,"date":"2026-09-14","range":"","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\【福利】9.14 检验题__阅读理解(1).docx","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"test","label":"检验题","file":"【福利】9.14检验题__完型填空(1).docx","ext":".docx","bytes":57708,"date":"2026-09-14","range":"","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\【福利】9.14检验题__完型填空(1).docx","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"test","label":"检验题","file":"【福利】9.14检验题__新题型__段落排序(1).docx","ext":".docx","bytes":70909,"date":"2026-09-14","range":"","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\【福利】9.14检验题__新题型__段落排序(1).docx","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"layout","label":"杂志排版","file":"9.14杂志排版.pdf","ext":".pdf","bytes":856366,"date":"2026-09-14","range":"","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\9.14杂志排版.pdf","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"}]};
